content.text:
I fixed the bug in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`. With scoped styles, an `@media` query nested inside `.foo` now overrides the base `background-color` as it should.

**Cause:** When a scoped rule has nested rules, the compiler moves the rule's own declarations into a `&[data-v-…]` block so they get the scope attribute. Declarations sitting directly inside a nested at-rule such as `@media` or `@container` were left out. They never got the attribute, so they lost to the scoped base declaration.

**Fix:** I added a helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`, that moves a node's direct declarations and comments into a `& { … }` block. It now runs on the scoped rule itself and on each at-rule nested directly inside it. The new `&` blocks then get the scope attribute like any other nested rule.

I compiled a test case close to the issue's (with an extra nested rule inside `@media`), and the output is now:

```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: red }
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: green }
.baz[data-v-test] { color: red }
}
}
```

The existing `compileStyle` tests still pass. I used a temporary test file for the check and deleted it afterwards, so no new test was added.

Two behaviour notes:
- Comments that sit next to those declarations are now moved into the `&` block along with them.
- Only at-rules directly inside the scoped rule are handled. Declarations in a second level of at-rule nesting (for example `@supports` inside `@media`) are not wrapped, so they still miss the scope attribute.

